\section{Direction}{
Arrowheads show the edge direction \strong{you declared} — from \code{source} to
\code{target} as supplied to \code{\link[=netis_data]{netis_data()}}. They are never drawn from the exposure
engine's canonical orientation, which is a derived convention for exposure
arithmetic and is transposed relative to the raw declaration for some
datasets. If the arrows look backwards, the declaration is what to check.
}

\section{Layouts}{
\code{"circle"} and \code{"grid"} are native, deterministic, and defined for any network.
\code{"mds"} is native but needs a finite geodesic distance matrix, so it refuses on
a disconnected network — including one with an isolate — rather than
substituting a finite value for an infinite distance. igraph layouts are
available by name when igraph is installed and error actionably when it is not;
rnetis never silently substitutes one layout algorithm for another.

Most igraph layouts are stochastic. They are drawn from \code{seed} (default
\code{1}), so the same call gives the same picture, and the session's random
stream is restored afterwards: drawing a plot does not change the caller's
next random number. \code{seed = NULL} draws from the session's stream instead,
advancing it like any other random draw. The seed used is recorded in the
\code{"network"} table's \code{layout_seed} attribute.
}

\section{Missing values}{
Nothing is dropped for being missing. Every node keeps its row and carries a
\code{missing_state}, so an \strong{isolate} (present, no ties, a real zero) stays
distinguishable from a node whose value was never observed. Where the object
cannot answer a question — whether a network was observed at a given wave is
sometimes not recoverable — the state is \code{undetermined} rather than a guess.

On the \code{"exposure"} panel this distinction is the whole point. Under the
default non-row-normalized sum an isolate's exposure is exactly \code{0}, because
summing over no alters is zero — a defined value, not a gap. A node one of
whose alters went unobserved has \code{NA}. The two are marked differently, and the
number of alters is computed rather than inferred from the value: a node \emph{with}
alters can also total exactly zero when their values cancel, so reading
"0 means isolate" off the number would be wrong precisely where it matters.
Alters are the present ties of \code{variable}, so a stored row that the
network's presence declaration says is not a tie is not counted. On the
\code{"centrality"} panel, likewise, only an isolate's zero is marked
\code{structural_zero}; a connected node that scores zero is an observed value.
}

\section{Multiple groups}{
Node labels are unique only within a group. The \code{"coverage"}, \code{"missing"},
\code{"exposure"} and \code{"centrality"} panels key every node by (group, node), and
label the axis by group and node when the data has several groups, so two
groups that both contain a node \code{"a"} are shown as two nodes.
}

\section{Errors and refusals}{
Drawing a network requires knowing which rows are ties. A network declared
\code{presence_mode = "complete_dyadic"} with no presence column and no value rule
has \strong{undetermined} presence, and this refuses rather than treating every
non-zero row as a tie — a measured zero-weight tie and a structurally absent
one are not the same thing. Declare \code{presence_col} or \code{presence_rule} via
\code{tie_meta} in \code{\link[=netis_data]{netis_data()}}.

A multi-group object refuses to draw a network without \code{group}: picking the
first one silently would look like a complete picture of the data.
}

